artifact.self_contained_problem:
A Hardy field is a closed-under-differentiation, field of germs at $+\infty$ of real-valued functions defined on some half-line $[t_0,\infty)$. Fix a Hardy field $\mathcal H$ and let $F=\{a_1,\dots,a_\ell\}\subset\mathcal H$ consist of functions of polynomial growth (i.e., for each $i$ there exists $k\in\mathbb N$ with $a_i(t)/t^k\to 0$). Denote by $\mathrm{span}^*(F)$ the set of all nontrivial real linear combinations $a=c_1a_1+\cdots+c_\ell a_\ell$ with $(c_1,\dots,c_\ell)\in\mathbb R^\ell\setminus\{0\}$.

Say that the family of integer-part sequences $\{([a_1(n)]),\dots,([a_\ell(n)])\}$ is *good for $\ell$-fold mean convergence (for a single transformation)* if for every invertible measure-preserving system $(X,\mathcal B,\mu,T)$ and every $f_1,\dots,f_\ell\in L^\infty(\mu)$, the averages
$$\frac1N\sum_{n=1}^{N} T^{[a_1(n)]}f_1\cdot T^{[a_2(n)]}f_2\cdots T^{[a_\ell(n)]}f_\ell$$
converge in $L^2(\mu)$ as $N\to\infty$.

Prove or disprove: the family $\{([a_1(n)]),\dots,([a_\ell(n)])\}$ is good for $\ell$-fold mean convergence if and only if every function $a\in\mathrm{span}^*(F)$ satisfies at least one of the following three asymptotic alternatives:
\begin{itemize}
\item[(i)] $|a(t)-c\,p(t)|/\log t\to\infty$ as $t\to\infty$ for every $c\in\mathbb R$ and every $p\in\mathbb Z[t]$;
\item[(ii)] $a(t)-c\,p(t)\to d$ as $t\to\infty$ for some $c,d\in\mathbb R$ and some $p\in\mathbb Z[t]$;
\item[(iii)] $|a(t)-t/m|\le C\log t$ for all sufficiently large $t$, for some nonzero $m\in\mathbb Z$ and some constant $C>0$.
\end{itemize}

